# Packages-Root — the allow-list for Runink River, the developer workstation (DEsk on
# Wayland, s6, encrypted ZFS root, one pinned kernel).
#
# artools installs profile.yaml's rootfs lists on top of its own desktop common.yaml; every
# package profile.yaml names must be listed here (river lint profile-manifest), and the
# closure of this list is checked against forbidden.closure (river lint closure).
#
# NOT on this image, deliberately: a Kubernetes node, NAT64, platform runtimes, model
# payloads. A downstream distribution that needs them builds its own profile from this
# repository's packaging (docs/BUILD.md, "Downstream distributions").

# ── base + boot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
base
linux-runink

mkinitcpio
grub
efibootmgr

# ── firmware ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A workstation fleet is not one SKU: the reference machine is AMD (Radeon, HawkPoint), and
# amdgpu will not initialise without its firmware split. Both vendors are listed so one image
# boots either.
linux-firmware-amdgpu
linux-firmware-intel
# NETWORK FIRMWARE FOR HARDWARE THAT IS NOT INTEL. Added 2026-10-18 after the
# owner booted the medium on real hardware and it recognised NO NIC and no
# wifi card. linux-firmware-intel carries iwlwifi, so an Intel machine was
# fine and the gap was invisible on the reference hardware; linux-firmware-
# amdgpu is a GPU split and carries no network firmware at all. Anything with
# a Realtek, Broadcom, MediaTek, Atheros or Qualcomm radio or NIC had nothing
# to load, so the kernel found no device and the installer's first screen --
# which is the network step -- had nothing to offer.
#
# These are the NETWORK splits only, not the whole linux-firmware. An
# INSTALLER medium has to come up on hardware nobody chose in advance, and a
# machine that cannot reach the network cannot be installed; a machine with an
# unaccelerated GPU still installs (the nomodeset entry exists for that). That
# asymmetry is why the GPU splits stay vendor-scoped and these do not.
#
# All six verified present in Artix [system] at PIN_DATE 2026-07-01 before
# being listed, so this cannot fail the build three hours in on a typo.
linux-firmware-realtek
linux-firmware-broadcom
linux-firmware-atheros
linux-firmware-mediatek
linux-firmware-qcom
linux-firmware-marvell

# ── ZFS root ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypted ZFS root (invariant 3); the datasets are created with compression=zstd by the
# installer.
runink-zfs
runink-zfs-utils

# ── installer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From the pinned [runink] local repo: river-hwprobe + river-plan (the
# install plan runink-install confirms by disk serial) and river-netsetup + river-netcheck
# (the network-first step the live session opens with).
runink-installer
# Live-medium boot and the installer's disk tools (the installed machine is a clone of the live
# rootfs, so they stay on it too).
squashfs-tools
# NOT mkinitcpio-nfs-utils (removed 2026-10-01). It ships `ipconfig` and `nfsmount` for
# mkinitcpio's `net` hook — NFS-root boot — and no initramfs this image builds names that
# hook: the live one comes from iso-initcpio's /etc/mkinitcpio-default.conf
# (base udev artix_shutdown artix artix_loop_mnt artix_kms modconf block filesystems
# keyboard keymap), the installed one from root-overlay/etc/mkinitcpio.conf.d/zfs.conf.
# Nothing in the resolved closure requires it, nothing in this repository referenced it, and
# docs/OWN-BASE.md already listed it as a drop.
artools-base
dialog
parted
gptfdisk
dosfstools

# ── s6 init ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# s6, NOT systemd. This is a hard invariant of the image and it constrains the desktop:
# every service below must have an s6 counterpart, which is why the -s6 packages are named
# explicitly rather than relying on a desktop meta-package to drag in systemd units.
s6
s6-rc
s6-linux-init
s6-scripts
elogind-s6
dbus-s6
networkmanager-s6
openssh-s6
# chrony + chrony-s6: the clock. NOTHING on this image synchronised time before 2026-10-02,
# and an unsynchronised clock is not a cosmetic problem: TLS certificates are rejected as
# not-yet-valid or expired, k0s/Kubernetes client certificates and tokens fail the same way,
# and GitHub App JWTs are refused with "'exp' claim is too far in the future" — which is how
# the self-hosted runner on an image built from this profile had every private-repo CI job
# 401 after drifting ~39s ahead of GitHub. chrony-s6 ships the `chrony` s6-rc bundle
# (chrony-srv + chrony-log) the way networkmanager-s6 ships NetworkManager; profile.yaml
# enables it by the bundle name.
chrony
chrony-s6
# bluez-s6 ships the `bluetoothd` s6-rc bundle (bluetoothd-srv + bluetoothd-log, depends on
# dbus); cups-s6 ships `cupsd`. Both are enabled in profile.yaml's live-session.services the
# same way sddm is, and 20-clone-rootfs.sh carries the live default bundle to the target.
bluez-s6
cups-s6

# ── network ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
networkmanager
wpa_supplicant
openssh
iptables
nftables

# ── desktop: DEsk on Wayland ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEsk (owner decision 2026-10-30; docs/DESKTOP-DESK-PLAN.md) replaced KDE Plasma: one
# wlroots compositor that is the whole desktop — windows as paper on a desk, the drawer, the
# bookcase, the post box (notifications), the tray and the front-door lock — built in
# [runink] from DEsk's release plus the Runink fork's patches. Plasma, KWin, SDDM, Breeze, Qt
# and KDE Frameworks left the image with it (forbidden.closure keeps them out).
runink-desk
runink-wlroots0.18
# The login screen: greetd, running gtkgreet fullscreen in cage, as an s6 service from
# runink-greetd-s6 (Artix has greetd but no greetd-s6). greetd itself comes from Artix's
# repositories for now (debt).
greetd
runink-greetd-s6
runink-cage
runink-gtkgreet
# sd-bus for DEsk's post box, tray and logind power (elogind's library, not systemd's).
libelogind
# What DEsk's session helper starts (its autostart entries) and what it opens. Each is a
# small GTK or C program; none is Python, which the image does not carry: so no udiskie
# (automounting and the low-battery letter are river-desk's, runink-installer), blueman
# (Bluetooth: runink-bluetuith, below), system-config-printer (CUPS's own pages on
# localhost:631) or ibus (152 MiB, Python setup).
mate-polkit
network-manager-applet
nm-connection-editor
# Bluetooth devices: bluetuith (Go, MIT) in a terminal, built in [runink]; DEsk's Bluetooth row
# opens it (river-bluetooth).
runink-bluetuith
gnome-keyring
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
xorg-xwayland
xdg-user-dirs
xdg-utils
wdisplays
# Print and Alt+Print (desk-screenshot): grim, the clipboard, and the letter saying where.
grim
wl-clipboard
libnotify
# DEsk's own typefaces (URW base 35).
gsfonts

# ── applications (daily desktop) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEsk's standard set: the classic GTK programs, which leave their frame to the desk and lie
# on it as paper. foot is the terminal (Wayland-native, under 1 MiB).
foot
thunar
tumbler
gvfs
udisks2
mousepad
ristretto
atril
engrampa
parole

# Browser. Its telemetry, studies and default-browser nag are switched off system-wide by
# root-overlay/etc/firefox/policies/policies.json (Mozilla enterprise policies).
firefox

# ── boot splash: Plymouth (workstation ONLY) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The RIVER `river` script theme (root-overlay/usr/share/plymouth/themes/river). Artix
# ships plymouth in [world] and its mkinitcpio hook has a non-systemd path (add_runscript;
# the busybox hook starts plymouthd), so it is s6-clean. Its dependencies (cairo, pango,
# fontconfig, freetype2, libx11, libdrm, libxkbcommon, xkeyboard-config, glib2) are
# already in this closure: measured with pactree (2026-09-22), plymouth adds only itself
# (the s6/Artix subtrees are unresolvable off-box; recheck on Artix).
# s6 has no plymouth-quit.service; root-overlay/etc/s6/adminsv/plymouth-quit does that job.
plymouth

# ── graphics: mesa ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# vulkan-radeon for the AMD reference machine, vulkan-intel so the same image is usable on
# Intel hardware. libva for hardware video decode.
mesa
vulkan-icd-loader
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-intel
libva-mesa-driver
mesa-utils

